WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.170
You've told yourself
you'd go outside today.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.210
It's 5:30 p.m. You didn't.
Not because you couldn't.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.590
Because nothing was
pulling you out the door.

00:00:12.882 --> 00:00:16.332
Hi, we are Team Sprinters. I'm
Vinod and my teammate is Ansh.

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:20.792
We are UW students building Roam, a social
walking app for the iStartup Launch

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:24.352
Sprint. And the problem that we
described, it's not a personal quirk.

00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:26.904
It's an epidemic. That's
why we've built Roam.

00:00:27.454 --> 00:00:34.404
It's the social walking app. When you open
it, Echo, a voice companion, greets you.

00:00:34.774 --> 00:00:37.044
Echo isn't a coach. It's a friend.

00:00:37.074 --> 00:00:41.056
It asks you what you ate today, notices
when you turn down a new street, and

00:00:41.086 --> 00:00:42.286
once says “great job.”

00:00:43.406 --> 00:00:47.076
As you walk, your city reveals
itself on a fog of war heat map.

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:49.936
Streets you've never
walked clear from the fog.

00:00:50.126 --> 00:00:51.816
Streets you walk again, glow warmer.

00:00:51.846 --> 00:00:53.396
Let me show you. This is Jordan.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.596
29, knowledge worker.
Hasn't been outside today.

00:00:57.686 --> 00:01:00.596
One tap. And it starts. Echo says hi.

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:04.656
Watch the fog lift. Each tile is
about 100 meters of real street.

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:06.396
The golden line is Jordan's path.

00:01:06.426 --> 00:01:10.956
Now let's meet Kobe. He's new to Seattle,
and he's traveling through Capitol Hill

00:01:10.986 --> 00:01:13.216
along with his two
friends, Priya and Marcus.

00:01:13.486 --> 00:01:15.916
Watch the colors deepen
as their paths overlap.

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:19.896
Each of them has a unique
number of tiles added in.

00:01:19.926 --> 00:01:22.116
The leaderboard tracks
who discovered what.

00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:26.711
And it's a friendly competition that makes
you want to go from one block to another.

00:01:27.041 --> 00:01:28.791
We did not build this in a vacuum.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:30.715
We've run customer interviews

00:01:30.765 --> 00:01:32.775
through every phase of the sprint.

00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:36.955
And our biggest learning? People
don't want another fitness app.

00:01:37.345 --> 00:01:38.545
They want a reason.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:42.175
No metrics, no goals.
Just, hey, let's go.

00:01:42.965 --> 00:01:45.195
And that insight changed everything.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:50.164
I think it's a pretty good idea, you know,
to make introverts, you know, to start

00:01:50.194 --> 00:01:53.537
going outside and start
exploring some areas.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:58.837
I think my favorite part of it is that if
you go to like a place like a bunch of

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:03.557
times it starts to change color and
I especially like that part of it.

00:02:03.597 --> 00:02:09.147
I think for me it would be, you know, pretty
fun to like compete with other people

00:02:09.177 --> 00:02:11.447
to try and explore the most.

00:02:11.663 --> 00:02:16.073
So we repositioned. Roam is not
just for lonely Jordan anymore.

00:02:16.223 --> 00:02:19.673
It's for Kobe, who's new to
the city, and an extrovert.

00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:22.946
It's for a group of friends who want
to explore a new place together.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.466
Is it AI?

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:27.166
A bit of it. A bit of AI? Yeah.

00:02:27.196 --> 00:02:30.493
Okay. I'm going to sit this one out,
but that was a really good pitch.

00:02:30.543 --> 00:02:33.498
You almost made AI sound
good to me, so good job.

00:02:33.528 --> 00:02:36.078
Yeah, I'll be looking out for it.

00:02:36.108 --> 00:02:37.168
Yeah, thanks a lot.

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:38.268
Roam,

00:02:38.298 --> 00:02:39.868
put your shoes on.
